Xiaoqing Chen wrote:
> 
> I have an Alpha 4100 running DU 4.0D. Use static routing.
> Sice a couple of weeks ago, I have very strange problem
> and Digital support does not have any clue either: the
> routing table is always changing!
> 
> First strange thing is that it has 2 network masks: the one
> I added and another one from somewhere else (no clue!).

?????? What does "ifconfig tu0" (I assume it is tu0) say?

> Then there are 2 default gateways: one is regular one and
> one I have no idea where it gets. If you remove it,
> it will come back after a few miniutes.

Default gateways only get in if you statically add them or a dynamic routing
protocol introduces one. There can be more than one default gateway.

> Then a routing path to local network: it will pick up a
> PC (runnning database client) which just boot as the
> gateway for local networks (so it is also changing from
> time to time).

??????? "Say WHAT?"

The route to your local LAN should be via interface on that LAN, like:

IP		Net mask		Interface
192.168.61	255.255.255.0	tu0

How do you access the network anyway?

> I checked the route daemon and gated and none of them are running.

Neither RouteD, nor GateD can cause this kind of behavior.

> I one time suspect the SNMP thing might cause the problem.
> But after I remove the SNMP service from Alpha, problem still exists.

I'm not familiar with SNMP, but I don't think it can cause such a mess. Anyway
that's not it.

> I have no clue at all. The server was ok for long time.

Are you sure that there is not something dumb, like DHCP, BootP or something
like that?
That's the only thing that comes to mind, regarding such problems.

Also, you could shutdown the network and related services (see /sbin/init.d/*
for that), then manually bring it up, like:

ifconfig tu0 <IP> netmask <net-mask> broadcast <b-cast>
route add default gw <d-gw>

And check your routing tables in all steps.

Nix.





