Peter deFriesse (peter@dev.null) wrote:
: }  I am looking for _anyone_ who has succeeded in in making a 
: }KZPBA-CA (Qlogic 1040) SCSI adapter work as a second/external
: }bus in a personal workstation au433.  With SCSI-2 devices, NOT
: }Ultra SCSI.  On the external port.
: }
: }  If you have done this, *please* contact me offline (or on).
: }"Me too's" welcome...
: }
:   Summary:

:   In order to run narrow/SCSI-2 devices on the external connector
: of the KXPBA-CA, you must purchase the BN36A-0B cable.  This is
: a 6-inch cable that terminates the high bits on the bus.

:   Beware if you are doing this.  If you buy one of these monsters,
: you're going to get socked for almost US $300 for the SCSI adapter
: and then (surprise, surprise) another US $190 for the cable (that
: is *not* a typo; the cable lists for that much).

Well, Qlogic QLA1040-BK kit can be obtained directly from Qlogic for 
about US$180 (qty 1). I am sure you may be able to find a better
price if you shop around. Some other isp1040-based cards by other
manufacturers also work, and may be significantly cheaper.

However, Qlogic's card does not work as is in DPWs. (Digital mucks 
with the configuration registers of the KZPBA, so that the standard 
firmware does not recognize the Qlogic's card). If you do not intend 
to boot from the card, the procedure needed to get DU to recognize
it has been described in alpha-osf-managers mailing list some time
ago. Basically, it involves extracting the RISC firmware from the
x86 boot PROM and converting it to a format understood by the DU
driver.

It is also possible to boot from unmodified Qlogic cards. However,
this involves reflashing the DPW's PROM with unofficial (and 
unsupported) version of the firmware.

Concerning the wide-to-narrow conversion: the Qlogic card contains
a built-in software-controlled terminator for the high eight bits
of the SCSI bus. If you do not intend to mix wide and narrow devices 
on the same card, it may be possible to avoid using the expensive
actively terminated wide-to-narrow adapters. The relevalt configuration
bits are documented on the Qlogic's web site, and may be modified
using the isp_edit command of the SROM console.

Even if you have to use the wide-to-narrow cable for whatever
reasons, these are pretty well standartized and do not need to 
be bought from Digital. 

And yes, I do agree with you that Digital's prices on commodity
components are absolutely insane.

Regards,

/Serge.P



