SNIP SNIP...

> The original routine in INN's nnrpd (1.7.*) is:
>
>          /* See if we should lookup a specific user in the passwd file */
>          if (user && pass && EQ(fields[2], "+")) {
>             if ((pwd = getpwnam(user)) == NULL)
>                 continue;
>             if (!EQ(pwd->pw_passwd, crypt(pass, pwd->pw_passwd)))
>                 continue;
>         }
>
> I've tried changing by adding:
>
> int auth_stat;
> char *argv[] = {"NNRPD",NULL};
>
> and using:
>
>          /* See if we should lookup a specific user in the passwd file */
>          if (user && pass && EQ(fields[2], "+")) {
>             auth_stat=sia_validate_user(NULL,1,argv,NULL,user,NULL,
>                 0,NULL,pwd->pw_passwd);

Don't pass pwd->pw_passwd - pass the argument 'pass' - it's the user's pass phrase
correct?  What you're passing is the password taken from the password database - if from
/etc/passwd and if user is in the Enhanced Database (auth.db), then what's in that field
could be "*".  In my previous entry you will note I said other UNIX's use getpwnam() and
crypt() themselves - with SIA and Enhanced Security the crypt() routine may not have
encrypted your password, so you cannot do this.  With SIA no matter what security
mechanism you've installed (Base, Enhanced, DCE, ASDU, etc.) use of the sia_*() routines
will guarantee you won't have to change your code depending on your user's
authentication model.


>             if (!EQ(auth_stat, "SIACORRECT"))

That's SIASUCCESS not SIACORRECT.  (see /usr/include/sia.h)

>
>                 continue;
>          }
>
> Snip.. .Snip...

