In article <6tsddt$bmg$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, jamesleetong@yahoo.com writes:
>Hi there,
>  I'am a newbie at digital unix, and I want to know if any of you have a
>feedback on its clustering solutions. I think digital was the first one who
>introduced it in UNIX, but I wonder if it offers better uptime that the high
>availability products of IBM/SUN/HP.
>
>  Any comments?
>

All the vendors have something they call clustering so they can bring it 
up when they're trying to sell into a VMS site.  Only digital's
TruCluster product has features like the distributed lock manager so
that 2 CPUs can directly access a dual pathed disk simultaneously, all
others rely on NFS services, disabling one path until failover.

I've yet to see a UNIX who's clustering makes managing a large number of
systems as simple as a VMScluster, but there are third party products
(like Tivoli) which address that issue directly instead of as a benefit
of clustering.

As far as uptime, just a single naked UNIX system will have a great
variation in reliability depending on the vendor and the release. 
Adding UNIX clustering may or may not affect this depending on what the
reliability problem is.

