Jeff Denham wrote:
> 
> Joe Mario wrote:
> 
> > Bill James wrote:
> > > I guess lots/all of us suffer from this - we customarily get VS over
> > > 300Mb and sometimes 400Mb while linking our 35Mb executable. 128Mb
> > > machine unusable during this period.
> > >
> > > Why is it so big???
> >
> > The amount of memory used by ld during a link is primarily a function of
> > the
> > number and size of the input objects and the number of external
> > symbols in those objects.  Other things come into play but to a smaller
> > degree.  The resultant size of the executable says nothing about what
> > the linker was dealing with for inputs.
> 
> In other words, ld is essentially mmap()-ing the whole
> shooting match -- .o's and referenced .a and .so --
> into its address space. This makes it much more efficient
> in terms of its I/O behavior, but as you're seen, can
> result in a huge VM footprint.
> 

I've noticed that it is tolerant of running out of swap space - you
get messages saying 'cannot allocate requested swap' or some such. I
guess that it starts dealing in I/O when that happens - perhaps I
can constrain it using 'limit'... no, just tried limit addressspace
and it died horribly... think again...

