> security manual makes it sound like you can just use sia_getpasswd.
> The table on page 20-3 lists sia_getpwnam(), though.  Or is there another
> function that then passes the call based on matrix.conf?
>

Don't try and mimic what someone else does - that is find out what the
username is, get an entry from the password database, ask for the password,
compare the result in the password db with a self crypt()'d change of the
user's input password.

SIA will do what you want it to do very simply if you don't try to follow
other examples...  The question you should be asking is why do you need to
get a passwd pointer?  What are you doing with it - and of course will what
you're doing work in an Enhanced Security environment (or shadow password db
for other unixes).

>
> Anyway, I'd love to see just one *good* example before I try to hack
> this in. I want to do user based security on a 4.0b Digital Unix system
> with the enhanced security package installed.  Any tips would be
> appreciated.

See the example in Chapter 20 "Using the SIA Interface" Example 20-3 -
"Session Processing Code"... It's basically what login.c does (more or
less)...

See appendix D for examples of reauthentication and su authentication

If you already have a username/password and all you're trying to do is
validate if they are correct, then why not use sia_validate_user().

I guess if I knew what it was you were trying to do more exactly, then I
could provide a more direct answer...


