>> Segmentation fault
>
>  I don't have an answer for you on your COFF version number strangeness but,
>I have had problems in the past with with some programs that I have written for
>DEC Unix 4.0x that produced similar error messages.  The suggestion from our
>Digital support was to compile with the -assume noaligned_objects.  In most
>cases this solved that error.  Hope this helps.


That will work, but it will make the code run way slow and possibly
obscure an actual coding error.. read on.....

Basically the alpha processor expects to do 64bit fetches all the
time.  When a pointer references something which can't be easily
fetched with a single memory access the processor generates an
unaligned trap and doesn't do the fetch.    The "noaligned_objects"
embeds code to go ahead and handle the alignment issue by invalidating
the data cache and fetching the required value across the misaligned
address(s).

Generally this is found when you reference a pointer into a structure
using a different type than the original reference.  Example:

struct fiz {
        char baz;
        short bar;
        short biz;
} foo;

main()
{
	long *biz;

        biz = (long *) &foo.bar;
        *biz = 1;
}



Get a copy of the alpha processor manual, it goes into great detail
on this activity.






