You can get way more complicated than this simple suggestion,
but 'ps -m' will show you all the kernel threads in a process.
For example

$ ps -mp 0

Will show all the internal kernel threads -- they hang
out in task 0. Maybe something like the TOP program
shows threads or can be easily modified to show
threads. It probably used the task_info and table
system calls, or maybe /proc, to get the scoop on all
processes.

