In article <35F96E1C.B3D1FE3C@bgl.psycha.upenn.edu>, Nicholas Guido <nick@bgl.psycha.upenn.edu> writes:

>I am going to do a tape backup of a DEC server soon.  I have never done
>this before.  I think that I will use the dump command something to the
>effect of.
>
>dump -Oun -f /dev/nrmt* -b 32/fs1
>
>I am simply wondering if there is a better way to do this or if there is
>anyhting that I should know before I start this process.

You should read the manual page for the particular command and
any system management documentation you can find on the Documentation
CDROM so you get the command right:

>dump -Oun -f /dev/nrmt* -b 32/fs1

On my screen that 'O' looks like the letter Oh instead of the number
zero.  The device name /dev/nrmt* will be expanded by the shell to
all the density codes and tape units on the system, where only
one is allowed.  There should be a space after the 32.

I'm also not sure if dump will translate the options correctly
if they're spread out like that.  You might have to write it:

dump -0unfb /dev/nrmt#{a,h,l,m} 32 /file-system

replacing '#' with the right unit number and {a,h,l,m} with the
appropriate density letter.

If it isn't obvious, you need to repeat the command for every
file system you want to backup.  If any of the file systems are
AdvFS you have to use vdump instead of dump.  You can use vdump
for any UFSs as well.

Aside from that you've got the right basic idea.
>
