> I am going to do a tape backup of a DEC server soon.  I have never done
> this before.  I think that I will use the dump command something to the
> effect of.
> 
> dump -Oun -f /dev/nrmt* -b 32/fs1

You mean "dump -0un -f /dev/nrmt0h -b 32 /dev/rz....?

And, of course, "/dev/rz..." must be unmounted first, or mounted read-only.
Backuping / and /usr tends to be a pain in the ass with this.

I would suggest using "vdump -0uNC <FS_mount_point>". Of course, the FS has to
be mounted. If you want to do on-line backups, you can do it only with AdvFS and
it's Clone_File_Set facility. VDump supports any FS type recognized by your
kernel, AdvFS, UFS, NFS, CDFS.

> I am simply wondering if there is a better way to do this or if there is
> anyhting that I should know before I start this process.

A better way for what purpose? Dump and vdump create an image of the file system
and that is the most appropriate way to do a system backup.

Nix.





