In article <6to3kc$n1m@lilith.uab.es>,
 "Marc" <mortega@seneca.uab.es> writes:
|> Hi everyone,
|> 
|> We are working with Digital Unix on a DEC WS 500au, and we use
|> memory-intensive process (such as dictionaries with up to 14 MB). Now we
|> need to change some system parameters because the dictionary process does
|> not work as we want, after 120 MB of VM the process terminates with a
|> memory-fault message.
|> There's some way to modify the system to allow such process? Which
|> parameters are involved? Do we have to recompile the kernel?

Hi,

we had a similar problem.  A solution was:

 [1] add (or adjust) the following in /etc/sysconfigtab:

proc:
        per-proc-data-size=2147483648
        max-per-proc-data-size=2147483648
        per-proc-address-space=2147483648
        max-per-proc-address-space=2147483648
        max-per-proc-stack-size=134217728
        per-proc-stack-size=33554432
        max-proc-per-user=256
        max-threads-per-user=1024

  [2] reboot

Have eventually a look at

	sysconfig -Q proc	# shows the maximum acceptable values
	sysconfig -q proc	# shows the current values

